+    // Focus is not visibility. This used to pause Unity whenever the window lost focus
+    // and resume it only if `_isUnityPaused` was set -- but it paused through
+    // view.pause(), which never sets that flag, so nothing ever resumed it. Anything
+    // that takes focus without leaving the app froze the player for good: the screen
+    // recorder's "Start recording?" dialog and its floating toolbar, the notification
+    // shade, a chat bubble. A frozen player draws black, so the next round opened on a
+    // black screen, and only restarting the app brought it back.
+    //
+    // Losing focus now leaves Unity running. Actually leaving the app is handled by
+    // onHostPause/onHostResume in the view manager, which pause and resume through
+    // ReactNativeUnity.pause()/resume() and keep the flag honest. Regaining focus
+    // resumes through the same call, so a player paused by any other route comes back.
+    if (hasWindowFocus) {
+      ReactNativeUnity.resume();
     }
